3. TYPES OF TREE
================================================================================================================

1. GENERAL TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A General Tree is a Tree in which a Node can have any number
   of Children. There is no fixed maximum limit on the number
   of Children.

   Example:

                              10
                         /    |    \
                        20    30    40
                       / \          |
                      50  60        70

   Explanation:

   - Node 10 has 3 Children.
   - Node 20 has 2 Children.
   - Node 40 has 1 Child.

   There is no fixed limit on the number of Children.


2. BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Binary Tree is a special type of Tree in which every Node
   can have at most TWO Children.

   These two Children are:

   1. Left Child
   2. Right Child

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \      \
                         40   50     60

   Explanation:

   - Node 10 has two Children.
   - Node 20 has two Children.
   - Node 30 has one Child.
   - Nodes 40, 50 and 60 have zero Children.


3. BINARY SEARCH TREE (BST)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Binary Search Tree is a special type of Binary Tree in which
   the Left Subtree contains smaller values and the Right Subtree
   contains greater values than the current Node.

   Example:

                              50
                            /    \
                           30     70
                          /  \   /  \
                         20  40 60  80

   Explanation:

   - Values smaller than 50 are present in its Left Subtree.
   - Values greater than 50 are present in its Right Subtree.
   - The same ordering rule is followed at every Node.

   Important:

   Left Subtree < Root Node < Right Subtree


4. AVL TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   An AVL Tree is a Self-Balancing Binary Search Tree in which
   the height difference between the Left and Right Subtrees
   of every Node is at most 1.

   Example:

                              30
                            /    \
                           20     40
                          /  \
                         10   25

   Explanation:

   AVL Tree automatically maintains its balance.
   If insertion or deletion makes the Tree unbalanced,
   rotations are performed to restore balance.


5. HEAP TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Heap is a Complete Binary Tree that follows a specific
   Heap Property.

   Two Types:

   1. Max Heap:
      The Parent Node is greater than or equal to its Children.

   2. Min Heap:
      The Parent Node is smaller than or equal to its Children.

   Max Heap Example:

                              90
                            /    \
                           70     80
                          /  \
                         40   50

   Explanation:

   Every Parent Node is greater than or equal to its Children.

   Therefore, this is a Max Heap.


