4. WHAT IS A BINARY TREE?
================================================================================================================

   Definition:

   A Binary Tree is a Non-Linear and Hierarchical Data Structure
   in which every Node can have at most TWO Children.

   These two Children are called:

   1. Left Child
   2. Right Child

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \   /  \
                         40  50 60  70

   Explanation:

   - 10 is the Root Node.
   - 20 is the Left Child of 10.
   - 30 is the Right Child of 10.
   - 40 and 50 are Children of 20.
   - 60 and 70 are Children of 30.

   Important:

   A Binary Tree Node can have:

   1. Zero Children.
   2. Only One Child.
   3. Exactly Two Children.

   A Node cannot have more than two Children.


================================================================================================================
5. STRUCTURE OF BINARY TREE NODE
================================================================================================================

   Definition:

   Every Binary Tree Node generally contains three parts:

   1. Data  : Stores the actual value.
   2. Left  : Stores the reference of the Left Child.
   3. Right : Stores the reference of the Right Child.

   Diagram:

                 +------------+------------+-------------+
                 |    Left    |    Data    |    Right   |
                 +------------+------------+-------------+

   Java Implementation:

   class Node
   {
       int data;
       Node left;
       Node right;

       Node(int data)
       {
           this.data = data;
           this.left = null;
           this.right = null;
       }
   }

   Explanation:

   - data stores the actual value of the Node.
   - left stores the reference of the Left Child.
   - right stores the reference of the Right Child.
   - If a Child does not exist, its reference is null.


================================================================================================================
6. TYPES OF BINARY TREE
================================================================================================================

1. FULL BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Full Binary Tree is a Binary Tree in which every Node
   has either ZERO or EXACTLY TWO Children.

   No Node can have only one Child.

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \
                         40   50

   Explanation:

   - Node 10 has exactly 2 Children.
   - Node 20 has exactly 2 Children.
   - Node 30 has 0 Children.
   - Nodes 40 and 50 have 0 Children.

   Every Node has either 0 or 2 Children.

   Therefore, it is a Full Binary Tree.


2. COMPLETE BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Complete Binary Tree is a Binary Tree in which all levels
   are completely filled except possibly the last level.

   The last level must be filled from LEFT TO RIGHT without gaps.

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \   /
                         40  50 60

   Explanation:

   - Level 0 is completely filled.
   - Level 1 is completely filled.
   - The last level is filled from left to right.
   - There are no gaps before an existing Node.

   Therefore, it is a Complete Binary Tree.


3. PERFECT BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Perfect Binary Tree is a Binary Tree in which every
   Internal Node has exactly TWO Children and all Leaf Nodes
   are present at the same Level.

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \   /  \
                         40  50 60  70

   Explanation:

   - Node 10 has exactly 2 Children.
   - Nodes 20 and 30 have exactly 2 Children.
   - All Leaf Nodes (40, 50, 60, 70) are at the same Level.
   - Every Level is completely filled.

   Therefore, it is a Perfect Binary Tree.


4. BALANCED BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Height-Balanced Binary Tree is a Binary Tree in which
   the difference between the heights of the Left and Right
   Subtrees of every Node is at most 1.

   Example:

                              10
                            /    \
                           20     30
                          /  \
                         40   50

   Explanation:

   At Node 10:

   Height of Left Subtree = 1
   Height of Right Subtree = 0

   Difference = 1 - 0 = 1

   At Node 20:

   Height of Left Subtree = 0
   Height of Right Subtree = 0

   Difference = 0

   Every Node has a height difference of at most 1.

   Therefore, this is a Balanced Binary Tree.


5. SKEWED BINARY TREE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Definition:

   A Skewed Binary Tree is a Binary Tree in which every Node
   has at most ONE Child.

   Two Types:

   A. LEFT SKEWED BINARY TREE

                              10
                             /
                            20
                           /
                          30
                         /
                        40

   Explanation:

   Every Node has only a Left Child and no Right Child.

   B. RIGHT SKEWED BINARY TREE

                         10
                           \
                            20
                              \
                               30
                                 \
                                  40

   Explanation:

   Every Node has only a Right Child and no Left Child.

   Important:

   A Skewed Binary Tree looks similar to a Linked List.


